# Tucker Tally settings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# To change a setting:
#   1. Copy this file to a new file called  .env   (same folder, nothing before the dot)
#        Windows (PowerShell):  Copy-Item .env.example .env
#        macOS / Linux:         cp .env.example .env
#   2. Remove the "#" in front of the setting you want, and edit its value.
#   3. Stop the app (Ctrl+C) and start it again with  npm start
#
# Every setting is optional. With no .env file at all the app runs fine, using
# the defaults shown here. Lines starting with "#" are ignored. Do not put
# spaces around the "=" and do not use quotes.
#
# The .env file stays on your laptop: it is never uploaded anywhere (it is
# listed in .gitignore).


# ---- Where the app listens ---------------------------------------------------

# The port number. Your phone opens  http://<laptop-address>:3000  (or the
# https address from Tailscale, which does not show the port).
# If the app says "port already in use", pick another number such as 3001.
# If you change this, also change it in the "tailscale serve" command.
# PORT=3000

# Which network connections to accept.
#   0.0.0.0     = this laptop and other devices on your home Wi-Fi (default).
#                 Needed so your phone can reach the app.
#   127.0.0.1   = only this laptop. Use this if you will ONLY use Tailscale
#                 (tailscale serve talks to the app from the laptop itself).
# The app is not meant to be reachable from the public internet. Never set up
# port forwarding on your router for it.
# HOST=0.0.0.0


# ---- Where your data lives ---------------------------------------------------

# Folder that holds the database file (tucker-tally.sqlite). Relative paths
# are relative to the app folder. Everything you log is in this one file.
# DATA_DIR=data

# Folder for automatic and manual backups of the database.
# For real safety, point this at a different drive or a synced folder
# (for example a folder inside OneDrive, iCloud Drive, Dropbox or a USB disk).
# BACKUP_DIR=backups

# How many backups to keep of each kind ("daily" and "manual").
# The oldest are deleted after this many.
# BACKUP_KEEP=14


# ---- Food databases (optional) -----------------------------------------------

# Open Food Facts (barcodes and packaged foods) needs no key and no account.
# It is free and community-run. Searches prefer Australian products first.
# To prefer another country, use its English name as Open Food Facts spells it,
# for example: new-zealand, united-kingdom, united-states, canada.
# Leave it empty to search the whole world.
# OFF_COUNTRY=australia

# USDA FoodData Central (generic foods such as "banana, raw") is free but asks
# you to request a key. Without a key the app still works: you just get
# Open Food Facts results and your own foods.
#   1. Go to https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/api-key-signup
#   2. Enter your name and email. The key arrives by email in a minute.
#   3. Paste it below (no quotes).
# The key is only ever sent to api.nal.usda.gov.
# USDA_API_KEY=

# How long (in milliseconds) the app waits for the online food databases
# before giving up and showing your own foods with a notice. 5000 = 5 seconds.
# ONLINE_TIMEOUT_MS=5000


# ---- HTTPS (needed for the camera) -------------------------------------------

# Phones only allow the camera and "Add to Home Screen" on https addresses.
# The recommended way is Tailscale (see the README): run
#     tailscale serve --bg 3000
# and you do not need anything in this section.

# The address your phone uses, if it differs from the default. This only
# changes the address shown in the start-up message and QR code.
# Example for Tailscale:  PUBLIC_URL=https://my-laptop.tail1234.ts.net
# PUBLIC_URL=

# Fallback without Tailscale: serve HTTPS directly from the app using a
# certificate you made with mkcert (see "Plan B" in the README). Give the full
# path of the two files. When both are set the app also listens on HTTPS_PORT.
# Windows paths can use forward slashes:  C:/Users/you/certs/laptop.pem
# HTTPS_CERT_FILE=certs/laptop.pem
# HTTPS_KEY_FILE=certs/laptop-key.pem
# HTTPS_PORT=3443


# ---- Login -------------------------------------------------------------------

# How many days a phone stays signed in after its last use. Every time you use
# the app the clock starts again, so in normal use you only type your PIN once.
# SESSION_DAYS=180


# ---- Advanced / testing (normally leave these alone) -------------------------

# Cost of the PIN hashing. Higher is slower for an attacker but also slower
# to log in. 12 is a good value; the automated tests lower it so they are fast.
# BCRYPT_ROUNDS=12

# Point the app at different food-database servers. Used only by the
# automated browser tests, which run against a pretend server.
# OFF_BASE_URL=https://world.openfoodfacts.org
# USDA_BASE_URL=https://api.nal.usda.gov
